*I'm building [Caffeine](https://github.com/Brickell-Research/caffeine_lang), a compiler written in Gleam that compiles service expectations to reliability artifacts.
This post documents how we release new versions of Caffeine across our various distribution channels.

***

## At The Beginning It Was Just a Single CICD Pipeline

The initial use case for Caffeine was to plug in to a CICD-esque Github Action at my day job to maintain our SLOs. In the ["Origin Story"](https://caffeine-lang.run/blog/origins) post I detail how we went from Terraform to a Ruby Terraform
generator, to an early Caffeine prototype in Ruby, to Caffeine itself. The workflow for developers was a bit like this:

*(1) Modify some simplified representation of SLO state in a Github Repo*

{.figure}
![Makeing Some Changes](/images/designing-our-release-process_make_some_changes.png)

I didn't use [the cafe](https://caffeine-lang.run/cafe) to write Caffeine here... but nothing is stopping you 🤔


*(2) Put up a PR.*

{.figure}
![Putting up a PR](/images/designing-our-release-process_put_up_a_pr.png)

Merging the PR to then kick off CICD.

*(3) CICD will execute the compiler against this input, generating and then applying the generated Terraform (with the terraform state living local to the repo).*

{.figure}
![Github Actions View](/images/designing-our-release-process_github_action.png)

Successful CICD executions.

*(4) Users then navigate and see the changes to SLOs in Datadog.*

{.figure}
![Datadog SLO View](/images/designing-our-release-process_datadog.png)

A random overview of where I was collecting a subset of SLOs generated from caffeine.

To more easily facilitate this effort, [I created a Github Action that enables folks to leverage this workflow](https://github.com/Brickell-Research/caffeine_lang_github_action) and I detail how it came to be in my ["Packaging Caffeine"](https://caffeine-lang.run/blog/packaging-caffeine)
blog post.

For better or worse, this Github Action just fetches the lates release, which for Caffeine, [is actually automated per a Github Action](https://github.com/Brickell-Research/caffeine_lang/blob/main/.github/workflows/release.yml) whenever I push a new release like so:

```bash
git tag v0.2.10
git push origin v0.2.10
```

So we were set! All use cases were covered by some automation. No brain, no headache. 

![Very nice gif](https://media3.giphy.com/media/v1.Y2lkPTc5MGI3NjExOTYwYWtoNnplM2d3dTN4M3NkbHBydXNlMDZjYmRma2V3MDgwMGxpaCZlcD12MV9pbnRlcm5hbF9naWZfYnlfaWQmY3Q9Zw/Od0QRnzwRBYmDU3eEO/giphy.gif)

