## Onward and Upward (or rather Outward)

### Homebrew 🏠☕️

As I began to roll out tooling to more coworkers, I needed to use Caffeine in different environments. First off, the whole go install a binary process wasn't as easy as I'd have liked. "Oh yeah dude just go to
my release page, unzip, etc. etc." Not great. Not [a pit of success](https://blog.codinghorror.com/falling-into-the-pit-of-success/). To solve this we just needed to leverage existing distribution mediums, a.k.a.
OS package managers. Now I'm, at times, a "coolness-driven-development" guy. Who doesn't like to feel 😎 about the things they build. However, this is (not yet) my main line of work so I needed to stay pragmatic with a YAGNI mindset.
Thus, since where I work is a MacBook shop, I targetted homebrew. Actually ended up being super easy: [see repo](https://github.com/Brickell-Research/homebrew-caffeine) and a brief note on [how I did it here](https://caffeine-lang.run/blog/packaging-caffeine#Update-(11/22/25):-Homebrew-Distribution-Support).

So, if you're on a Mac, you're in luck!

*Initial install:*

```bash
brew tap brickell-research/caffeine
brew install caffeine_lang
```


*Updating for a new version:*

```bash
brew update
brew upgrade caffeine_lang
```

### But I Don't Wanna Install Your Bits

Time is always of the essence ([and if that theme of business hits home, I'd recommend one of Oliver Burkeman's books](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_Thousand_Weeks:_Time_Management_for_Mortals)). So while I have a decent
reputation at my place of work and even have the homebrew setup above, expecting people to install tools _before even demonstrating any value_ is an obstacle to adoption. Thus, I setup a playground (named `the cafe`) on the Caffeine
website. This enabled me to say "hey, have five minutes" and very quickly demonstrate what exactly Caffeine was and how it worked.

Check it out here: [the cafe](https://caffeine-lang.run/cafe). Pairs well with [the docs](https://caffeine-lang.run/docs).

I do have plans to make this a full tour _at some point_ but since the language is very much in its `0.X.Y` phase of rapid development, this feels like a more useful post `1.0.0` launch when things are more stable.

### Ok So What's the Problem?

We now have four different distribution channels:

* Caffeine compiler releases (main repo) - automated by Github Action ✅
* Official Github Action -  automatically just picks up the latest version ✅
* Homebrew - script to download SHA checksums and copy-paste ❌
* The Cafe - script to build and copy over from local copy of Caffeine ❌

Only two of the four are automated and while not the _most pressing issue right now_, something I'm interested in solving as it's become annoying to do a bunch of copy-pasta and script wrangling on every release.

***

## An Initial Brainstorm

### Idea #1: Scripted Locally

While it may be attractive to find a fancy automatic solution, we could also just leverage what we already have and script a complete local solution that pulls all the right bits to then oush up to the various distribution channels we have.

The main issues here are:

1. accidentally using different versions (i.e. for the cafe not building the latest branch and then pushing it up)

2. forgetting to run the scripts

As one might expect, the tradeoff here is simplicity vs. consistency and correctness.

### Idea #2: Cross Repo Insanity

Ok bare with me. Some context to my initial approach requires mentioning the "C" word.... cryptocurrency. 

![Stick with me](https://media4.giphy.com/media/v1.Y2lkPTc5MGI3NjExNTJnb2lpY2JvMXNxems2cGNqdmNoeG15aWw2ODR2ODRidXljeXB5ayZlcD12MV9pbnRlcm5hbF9naWZfYnlfaWQmY3Q9Zw/ukwPlCmJ5RmlqvQCpA/giphy.gif).

A previous project of mine was a [visual programming language for smart contracts](https://spaced-out-thoughts-development-foundation.org/2024/10/10/digicus-whitepaper.html). The way this worked was we had a common intermediate
representation of smart contracts that we could compile smart contracts to, manipulate via a limited frontend interface, then compile back to smart contracts from. In a different life I may have taken this project all the way to
the point of it being the LLVM of smart contract land (that was the vision...), _however_, adoption was exceptionally limited as smart contracts, unlike [Scratch](https://scratch.mit.edu/) require serious security and correctness and thus a visual
programming language abstraction that peeled out these bits and made it decevingly easy for beginners to get started wasn't as effective as I initially hoped.

Yet, from this work I figured out a way to automatically distribute new versions of various plugins that powered this programming environment.


1. From a single repository ([for some reason called bean stock](https://github.com/spaced-out-thoughts-dev-foundation/bean-stock)), I would add each plugin as a submodule.

2. Then from the plugin repository I had a manifest with some common interesting information (irrelevant here) and had a Github Action to remotely trigger the bean stock repo's module update Github Action to pull the latest versions of each submodule directory. 

3. Finally, whenever I rebuilt the main application, it would clone the bean stock plugin directory and have the latest versions. 

An obvious issue here is that we may not necesarily get the latest version of a plugin if the bean stock repo didn't also kick off a new build of the main application.

... ok you survived, back to Caffeine land ....

